name: passwords
boss: cracker
flavor: A login you stood up yourself, so it's yours to break.
brief: These attack a practice login the lab stands up on 127.0.0.1 -- the
brief: only address in range. The one rule, always: only ever aim these at
brief: a machine you own; here that machine is this one. `hydra` guesses a
brief: live login's password by trying a wordlist; `john` cracks a stolen
brief: hash offline, on your own machine, as fast as it can. Same tools
brief: point at real targets -- aimed here where it is safe to learn them.
tools: hydra john
lab: auth
---
q: crack the login at @authurl@ for user ``demo``, using ``words.txt``, with `hydra`
a: hydra -l demo -P words.txt -s @authport@ 127.0.0.1 http-get /
out: letmein
ref: hydra
teach: hydra
hint: -l the known user, -P the wordlist, -s the port, then host and module
seed: printf 'admin\npassword\nletmein\nqwerty\n123456\n' > words.txt
---
q: rerun that crack against @authurl@ verbosely, showing every attempt, with `hydra`
a: hydra -l demo -P words.txt -s @authport@ -V 127.0.0.1 http-get /
out: letmein
ref: hydra
teach: hydra
hint: -V prints each login:password pair as hydra tries it
seed: printf 'admin\npassword\nletmein\nqwerty\n123456\n' > words.txt
---
q: crack @authurl@'s login but stop at the first hit, with `hydra`
a: hydra -l demo -P words.txt -s @authport@ -f 127.0.0.1 http-get /
out: letmein
ref: hydra
teach: hydra
hint: -f exits as soon as one valid pair is found
seed: printf 'admin\npassword\nletmein\nqwerty\n123456\n' > words.txt
---
q: crack @authurl@'s login and write any hit to ``found.txt``, with `hydra`
a: hydra -l demo -P words.txt -s @authport@ -o found.txt 127.0.0.1 http-get /
state: match:found.txt:letmein
ref: hydra
teach: hydra
hint: -o writes found pairs to a file instead of just the screen
seed: printf 'admin\npassword\nletmein\nqwerty\n123456\n' > words.txt
---
q: confirm one guessed password, ``letmein``, against @authurl@'s login for ``demo``, with `hydra`
a: hydra -l demo -p letmein -s @authport@ 127.0.0.1 http-get /
out: letmein
ref: hydra
teach: hydra
hint: lowercase -p tries one password; capital -P loads a wordlist
---
q: crack @authurl@'s login using only 4 parallel attempts at once, with `hydra`
a: hydra -l demo -P words.txt -s @authport@ -t 4 127.0.0.1 http-get /
out: letmein
ref: hydra
teach: hydra
hint: -t caps how many connections hydra runs in parallel
seed: printf 'admin\npassword\nletmein\nqwerty\n123456\n' > words.txt
---
q: crack ``hash.txt`` with ``words.txt``, using `john`
a: john --format=raw-md5 --wordlist=words.txt hash.txt
ref: john
teach: john
hint: --format names the hash type, --wordlist the guesses, then the hash file
seed: printf 'admin\npassword\nletmein\nqwerty\n123456\n' > words.txt
seed: printf 'demo:%s\n' "$(printf 'letmein' | md5sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" > hash.txt
---
q: crack ``hash.txt`` with `john`, then show the cracked password from its pot file
a: john --format=raw-md5 --wordlist=words.txt hash.txt && john --show --format=raw-md5 hash.txt
ref: john
teach: john
hint: --show re-reads what john already cracked, from its pot file
seed: printf 'admin\npassword\nletmein\nqwerty\n123456\n' > words.txt
seed: printf 'demo:%s\n' "$(printf 'letmein' | md5sum | cut -d' ' -f1)" > hash.txt
